            <ArticleTitle>Hyperstress and outcomes in a long-term smoking intervention program.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>227-35</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A group of 182 smokers in a CHD risk factor intervention program were prospectively followed for 4 years to note the relationship of selected psychosocial factors to long-term outcomes in smoking cessation. The results of the study suggest that the combined effects of high levels of presumed stress and low self-reliance (measured by a multimode method) have predictive value in determining failure in long-term follow-up. The results were linked to a possible treatment-person interaction and to current theory on conditioned emotional states and addictive smoking.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
